nd{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$" 13194139534677 17592186045417 true] [17592186135058 1064 "The **QR factorization** of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns is the matrix product\n\n$$\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{A}\n=\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{Q} \\times \\underset{{\\large n} \\times {\\large n}}{R},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n\n- $R$ is an $n \\times n$ upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its diagonal.\n\nFor example, the $3\\times 2$ matrix\n\n![](images/b7/b71bb9b1dc9aa6d23f2c990052e2e83eeda6000fc64a61ccdb614669d840d38a.svg)\n\nhas the QR factorization\n\n![](images/7c/7c5c30e4011efe0ac87f02217dd1f52c6e64658c6e90d22ad8689209badeb1f4.svg)" 13194139534677 17592186045417 true] [17592186135058 1064 "The **QR factorization** of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns is the matrix product $$\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{A} = \\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{Q} \\times \\underset{{\\large n} \\times {\\large n}}{R},$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $Q$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n- $R$ is an $n \\times n$ upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its diagonal.\n\nFor example, the $3\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nhas the QR factorization\n\n$$\\ove